The function dfs implements depth first search. It takes as input an adjacency list and a starting vertex. It also takes a reference to the vector output : each visited vertex will be appended to output when dfs leaves that vertex. Note that we use the function dfs both in the first and second step of the algorithm. In the first step, we pass in the adjacency list of $G$ , and during consecutive calls to dfs , we keep passing in the same 'output vector' order , so that eventually we obtain a list of vertices in increasing order of exit times. In the second step, we pass in the adjacency list of $G^T$ , and in each call, we pass in an empty 'output vector' component , which will give us one strongly connected component at a time.

