We define the set $S(x, i)$ of subsets of $x$ as follows:

S(x, i) = \{y \subseteq x \mid y \oplus x < 2^i\}

In simpler terms, $S(x, i)$ contains all subset masks of $x$ whose bits match those of $x$ except for the rightmost $i$ bits. S(\mathbf{101}1010, 4) = \{\mathbf{101}0000,\mathbf{101}0010,\mathbf{101}1000,\mathbf{101}1010\}

We can decompose $S(x, i+1)$ as follows:

1. If the $i$-th bit of $x$ is $0$, then $S(x, i+1) = S(x, i)$. 2. If the $i$-th bit of $x$ is $1$:

- Subsets with the $i$-th bit $1: S(x, i)$. - Subsets with the $i$-th bit $0 : S(x \oplus 2^i, i)$. S(x, i+1) = S(x, i) & \text{if the $i$-th bit is 0} \\ S(x, i) \cup S(x \oplus 2^i, i) & \text{if the $i$-th bit is 1}

Using the above partitioning, we define a DP table $dp[x][i]$ where: dp[x][i] = \sum_{y \in S(x, i)} A[y]

**Time Complexity:** $\mathcal{O}(N \cdot 2^N)$

Base case ($n=1$):

A^1 = \begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0\end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}F_2 & F_1 \\ F_1 & F_0\end{bmatrix}

Inductive step ($n+1$):

1 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 F_{n+1} & F_{n} \\ F_{n} & F_{n-1} F_{n+1}+F_n & F_{n}+F_{n-1} \\  F_{n+1} & F_{n} F_{n+2} & F_{n+1} \\ F_{n+1} & F_{n}

With this, we've shown that the formula holds for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

